## Avoid Sending SMS to Landlines in PHP with Twilio's Lookup v2 API


SMS is one of the most effective ways of staying in touch with your customers. This is because (among other reasons) it's notreliant on an internet connection, works on every mobile phone, and[has an](https://www.forbes.com/councils/forbestechcouncil/2021/04/14/the-anatomy-of-sms-engagement/)[open rate of 98%](https://www.forbes.com/councils/forbestechcouncil/2021/04/14/the-anatomy-of-sms-engagement/) .


But, when sending notifications via SMS in your PHP applications, how do you filter out landlines so that you don't pay for failed SMS? The phone number alone doesn't provide that information.


So, in this tutorial you'll learn how to filter out landlines in PHP using the power of[Twilio's Lookup v2 API](https://www.twilio.com/docs/lookup/v2-api) .


## How will the code work?


In essence, there's very little to it. The application will take an array of phone numbers and iterate over them. While iterating, each phone number's details will be retrieved using Twilio's Lookup v2 API. If it's a landline it will be skipped. Otherwise, it will be sent a short, fun, SMS. In addition, the application will log details of each phone number and if it was filtered or not, using Monolog.


## Prerequisites


To follow along with this tutorial, you'll need the following:


- A Twilio account (whether free or paid).[Create one now](https://twilio.com/try-twilio) if you don't have one already.
- A[Twilio phone number](https://www.twilio.com/en-us/phone-numbers) that can send SMS
- PHP 8.4 or above
- A phone number that can receive SMS and a landline number (preferrably, one that belongs to you or a friend)
- Your preferred text editor or IDE for writing PHP. I recommend Neovim and Visual Studio Code


### Create the basic project structure


You need to create the basic project file and directory structure, wherever you store your PHP projects. To do that, and change into the new project directory, run the following commands.


Copy code


```text
mkdir -p filter-landlines-in-php/src/Iterator
cd filter-landlines-in-php
```


If you're using Microsoft Windows, you don't need the` -p` argument to the mkdir command.


### Add the required libraries


Next, the project will need a few dependencies, these are:


- [Monolog](https://seldaek.github.io/monolog/) : Twilio's de facto logging library
- [Twilio's PHP Helper Library](https://www.twilio.com/docs/libraries/reference/twilio-php/index) : This simplifies interacting with Twilio's APIs
- [PHP Dotenv](https://github.com/vlucas/phpdotenv) : This loads environment variables from *.env* files to PHP's` $_ENV` and` $_SERVER` superglobals. **Note:** it's ideal for development environments, but shouldn't be used in production.


To install them, run the following command.


Copy code


```text
composer require monolog/monolog twilio/sdk vlucas/phpdotenv
```


### Add an autoloader namespace


With a *composer.json* file created by the previous command, you next need to add[a PSR-4 autoload mapping](https://getcomposer.org/doc/04-schema.md#psr-4) . This is required to store the code that filters landlines (and any other number that isn't a mobile/cell number) in a reusable class. It could be included directly in the main code, but that would make that code a little hard to read.


Add a mapping named` App` which maps to the *src* directory, by adding the following at the end of the existing configuration in *composer.json* .


Copy code


```text
"autoload": {
"psr-4": {
"App\\": "src/"
}
}
```


Then, run the following command to update Composer's auto-generated autoloader.


Copy code


```text
composer dump-autoload
```


### Set the required environment variables


Next, set two environment variables (your Twilio credentials), which are required to make authenticated requests to Twilio. To do that, in the project's top-level directory, create a new file named *.env* and paste the configuration below into the new file.


Copy code


```text
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER=
```


Then,[open the Twilio Console](https://1console.twilio.com/) in your browser of choice, and copy the **Account SID** , **Auth Token** from the **Workbench** at the bottom of the page.


Now, set the copied values as the values of` TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID` and` TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN` respectively, in *.env* .


Then, under **Quick actions** click **Phone numbers** , and copy an[E.164](https://www.twilio.com/docs/glossary/what-e164#regex-matching-for-e164) -formatted phone number from the **Phone Number** column. Paste the phone number as the value of , and` TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER` in *.env* .


### Write the PHP code


Now, it's time to write the PHP code. The code will be split across two files: *index.php* and *src/Iterator/MobilePhoneNumberFilterIterator.php* . Starting with the latter, create a new file in *src/Iterators* named *MobilePhoneNumberFilterIterator.php* , and paste the code below into the file.


Copy code


```text
<?php


declare(strict_types=1);


namespace App\Iterator;


use FilterIterator;
use Iterator;
use Psr\Log\LoggerInterface;
use Twilio\Exceptions\TwilioException;
use Twilio\Rest\Client;


final class MobilePhoneNumberFilterIterator extends FilterIterator
{
public const string NUMBER_TYPE_MOBILE = "mobile";


public function __construct(
Iterator $iterator,
private readonly Client $twilioClient,
private readonly LoggerInterface $logger,
) {
parent::__construct($iterator);
$this->rewind();
}


public function accept(): bool
{
$number = $this->getInnerIterator()->current();
assert(is_string($number));


$phoneNumber = $this->twilioClient
->lookups
->v2
->phoneNumbers($number)
->fetch(
[
"fields" => "line_type_intelligence",
],
);


try {
$type = $phoneNumber?->lineTypeIntelligence['type'] ?? "not available";
return $type === self::NUMBER_TYPE_MOBILE;
} catch (TwilioException $e) {
return false;
}
}
}
```


The class extends[FilterIterator](https://www.php.net/manual/en/class.filteriterator.php) from the[Standard PHP Library](https://www.php.net/manual/en/book.spl.php) (SPL). Iterators simplify traversing over objects using PHP's standard looping constructs, e.g., for, foreach, and while.` FilterIterator` , as the name implies, filters out unwanted values from an iterator, as they're iterated over through the logic in the class'` accept()` function.


The version above fetches details of each phone number, including[Line Type Intelligence](https://www.twilio.com/docs/lookup/v2-api/line-type-intelligence) data as it provides the line's type, e.g., *landline* , *mobile* , and *fixed VoIP* . If the number's line type is set to "mobile" the function returns` true` , keeping the number. Otherwise,` false` is returned, filtering out the number.


Next, create the application's entrypoint, *index.php* , in the project's top-level directory, and paste the code below into the file.


Copy code


```text
<?php


declare(strict_types=1);


use App\Iterator\MobilePhoneNumberFilterIterator;
use Monolog\{Level,Logger,Handler\StreamHandler};
use Twilio\Rest\Client;


require_once("vendor/autoload.php");


$dotenv = Dotenv\Dotenv::createImmutable(__DIR__);
$dotenv->load();


$phoneNumbers = ["<A MOBILE PHONE NUMBER>", "<A LAND LINE NUMBER>"];
$twilioPhoneNumber = $_ENV["TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER"];
$twilioClient = new Client(
$_ENV["TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID"],
$_ENV["TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN"]
);


$logger = new Logger('name')->pushHandler(
new StreamHandler('php://stdout', Level::Debug),
);


$iterator = new MobilePhoneNumberFilterIterator(
new ArrayIterator($phoneNumbers),
$twilioClient,
$logger,
);
foreach ($iterator as $number) {
$logger->debug(sprintf('Sending SMS to %s', $number));
$message = $twilioClient->messages->create(
$number,
[
"body" => "This is the ship that made the Kessel Run in fourteen parsecs?",
"from" => $twilioPhoneNumber,
],
);
}
```


Then, replace <A MOBILE PHONE NUMBER>


with a valid mobile/cell phone number, and <A LAND LINE NUMBER>


with a landline number.


The code loads[Composer's autoload file](https://getcomposer.org/doc/01-basic-usage.md#autoloading) ( *vendor/autoload.php* ) and registers several variables:


- **An array of phone numbers.** This is the list of phone numbers to filter and send SMS to.
- **Your Twilio phone number.** This tells Twilio which number to send the SMS from
- **A Twilio (Rest) Client object.** This is the central object in Twilio's PHP helper library.
- **A Monolog object.** This simplifies instrumenting the application, letting us know which numbers were able to receive SMS and which weren't. It will log information to *app.log* in the project's top-level directory. However, feel free to[change this location](https://seldaek.github.io/monolog/doc/02-handlers-formatters-processors) .
- **A MobilePhoneNumberFilterIterator object.** As previously discussed, this will filter landlines (and any phone number type other than mobiles) from a list of phone numbers. It's initialised with an[ArrayIterator](https://www.php.net/manual/en/class.arrayiterator.php) , itself initialised with an array of phone numbers to filter, and the Twilio` Client` object.


Then,` MobilePhoneNumberFilterIterator` is iterated over, and an SMS is sent to any non-landline number returned.


## Test that the code works as expected


With the code written and ready to run, it's time to see it in action. To do that, run the command below in a new terminal tab or session.


Copy code


```text
php index.php
```


If successful, you should see output similar to the following written to the terminal's output.


Copy code


```text
[2026-10-07T00:03:11.748349+00:00] name.DEBUG: Sending SMS to +61123456789 [] [
```


## That's how to avoid sending SMS to landlines in PHP with Twilio's Lookup v2 API


Thanks to[Twilio's Lookup v2 API](https://www.twilio.com/docs/lookup) and PHP's standard library, it's almost trivial to filter landlines (and other phone number types) from a list of phone numbers. Make sure you use this functionality to avoid being charged for failed SMS when integrating with Twilio.


